Your intuition becomes much sharper when we replace “many universes” with **many enacted worlds**: distinct centres of relevance, sensation and agency occupying one interacting reality. Tantrāloka then supplies a surprisingly detailed forecast of what science may discover after predictive processing.

# Nature Is a Parliament of Worlds

## What the Tantrāloka May Already Know About the Future of Biology and Consciousness Science

Every organism inhabits a different universe.

Not a separate cosmological universe, sealed away in another dimension, but a distinct **world of relevance**. A bacterium lives among chemical gradients. A plant lives among light directions, moisture, gravity, injury and seasonal rhythms. A tick inhabits a world dominated by temperature, odour and the possibility of blood. A human inhabits colours, faces, promises, gods, debts, stories and imagined futures.

These worlds overlap physically, but they are not phenomenally identical.

The chemical construction of an organism determines what it can register, what it must avoid, what counts as nourishment, how far into the future it can act and what forms of change it can survive. Contemporary work on diverse intelligence increasingly treats cognition as a graded capacity found across biological, artificial and hybrid systems rather than as something that suddenly begins with a human brain. Recent proposals describe agents as navigating physiological, morphological, behavioural and abstract problem-spaces, while a 2026 framework compares different forms of cognition across entire “cognition spaces.” ([arXiv][1])

Nature is therefore not one neutral world observed imperfectly by many creatures.

It is a field in which innumerable agents carve out different worlds through their:

* boundaries;
* sensory capacities;
* chemical needs;
* memories;
* possible actions;
* temporal horizons;
* social relationships.

The collaboration, competition and interpenetration of these worlds is what we call **nature**.

But this insight creates a deeper problem.

Modern science can increasingly describe how an organism constructs a model, protects a boundary, anticipates danger and coordinates its parts. Yet even a complete description of those functions seems to leave something out:

> Why is there a world *for* the organism?

Why does chemical damage hurt rather than merely trigger avoidance? Why does red appear? Why is a recursively modelled world present instead of dark?

Predictive processing does not finish the story. It identifies the machinery of finite world-construction. Tantrāloka begins where that story reaches its limit.

