# 2. The three things modern theory keeps separating

Modern theories tend to specialise in one of three dimensions.

Physics describes **structure**.

Consciousness studies investigate **presence**.

Biology and cognitive science investigate **agency**.

Abhinavagupta treats these as inseparable aspects of one reality:

```text
prakāśa      — manifestation, illumination, presence
vimarśa      — reflexive apprehension and self-relation
svātantrya   — power, freedom, capacity for manifestation
```

Sthaneshwar Timalsina emphasises that *vimarśa* is not merely a passive awareness observing itself. It synthesises modalities, enables knowledge and grounds the expressive first-person “I.” Consciousness in this tradition is not a mental item floating above matter; mentation and materiality are modes within a more fundamental reflexive process. ([Academia][5])

This produces a stronger starting point than either reductive physicalism or simple idealism:

> Reality is not dead structure to which presence is mysteriously added.
> Nor is it private experience dreaming an arbitrary physical world.
> It is structured manifestation possessing different degrees and modes of reflexive power.

Externally, such a process appears as causal organisation.

Internally, it appears as a world.

Agentively, it attempts to preserve, expand or transform that world.

This is the core of what I would call **reflexive structural monism**.

