# 3. Predictive processing has reached Māyā

Predictive-processing theories model perception as generative inference.

The organism does not wait passively for a completed world to enter through its senses. It generates predictions about hidden causes and updates those predictions through error signals. Action can also change sensory input, making perception and behaviour parts of a continuous loop.

The world we experience is therefore a controlled construction constrained by something beyond the construction.

That is already close to a modern scientific description of **Māyā**—provided Māyā is not mistranslated as complete unreality.

Māyā is the power by which an undivided field becomes available through distinctions:

```text
self / environment
inside / outside
valuable / harmful
possible / impossible
past / future
known / unknown
```

Without these distinctions, an organism could not exist.

A completely unbounded being would have no local metabolism, no particular sensory organs, no individual memory and no specific action to perform. Finitude is not merely a failure. It is what allows a determinate world to appear.

Chris Fields’ work on reference frames and boundaries reinforces this. Measurement requires a physically implemented frame that determines which differences count as signals and how those signals are interpreted. Observation is always contextual because a bounded system cannot interact with reality except through a finite interface and a limited repertoire of distinctions. ([arXiv][6])

The modern organism already looks remarkably like consciousness under the five *kañcukas*:

| Kañcuka    | Traditional limitation              | Scientific translation                          |
| ---------- | ----------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |
| **niyati** | localisation and causal restriction | embodiment, boundary and environmental coupling |
| **kāla**   | sequential time                     | finite memory and predictive horizon            |
| **vidyā**  | limited knowledge                   | sensory bandwidth and finite generative model   |
| **kalā**   | limited agency                      | action repertoire and controllable variables    |
| **rāga**   | desire arising from incompleteness  | preferences, valence and viability requirements |

Together these produce not merely an organism but a **world-for-an-organism**.

A useful formal sketch would be:

```text
Perspective = Boundary
            + Values
            + Model
            + Temporal horizon
            + Action-space
            + Reflexive access
```

Science currently has increasingly sophisticated theories of the first five.

Tantrāloka’s “spoiler” is that the sixth—reflexive presence—cannot simply be assumed to emerge automatically once the model is complicated enough.

