# 4. The next discovery: boundaries are apertures, not walls

Biology often speaks as if an organism first exists and then interacts with its environment.

Levin’s research points toward the reverse:

> The organism exists because interaction has been organised into a boundary-maintaining pattern.

Cells cooperate through biochemical and bioelectric communication. Gap junctions allow neighbouring cells to share electrical states, coordinate responses and participate in large-scale anatomical goals. Bioelectric networks can bind cell-level competencies into collective morphogenetic intelligence capable of building, repairing and maintaining bodies. ([PMC][7])

A boundary therefore has two functions:

1. it prevents the system from dissolving;
2. it selectively connects the system to what lies beyond it.

A perfectly impermeable boundary would protect nothing, because the enclosed system could not obtain energy, information or corrective feedback.

The next science of selfhood will understand boundaries not as walls but as **intelligent apertures**.

This is deeply Tantric.

Contraction does not sever finite consciousness from reality. It creates a perspectival opening through which reality manifests in one particular form.

> *svecchayā svabhittau viśvam unmīlayati*
> “By her own freedom, consciousness unfolds the universe upon her own screen.”

The body is not a prison into which consciousness has fallen.

It is an aperture through which a particular world becomes possible.

