# 6. Rāga: before intelligence comes caring

Predictive-processing accounts sometimes make organisms sound like detached statisticians updating probabilistic models.

But living systems do not merely estimate.

They care.

A glucose concentration is not just data for a cell. It has significance relative to metabolism. Tissue damage is not merely unexpected input. It threatens the continued integrity of an agentive system.

This is where *rāga* becomes much more profound than “desire” in the ordinary sense.

Rāga is the asymmetry by which one possible state matters more than another.

Without such asymmetry there is:

* no error;
* no goal;
* no danger;
* no need to act;
* no organismic perspective.

A model without preference describes possibilities.

A living model inhabits a landscape of attraction and aversion.

Alan Wallace and contemplative traditions are correct that a complete consciousness science must take first-person affect seriously, although Wallace’s stronger claims about substrate consciousness and post-mortem continuity remain outside established scientific evidence. Contemplative science’s defensible contribution is methodological: trained first-person observation may disclose patterns of attention, affect and self-construction that third-person measurement alone cannot identify.

The Tantric prediction is that **valence will turn out to be more fundamental than representation**.

An organism does not first build a neutral model and later add feelings.

Its world-model is organised from the beginning around what supports, threatens or expands its mode of being.

Chemistry becomes experience not merely by carrying information but by establishing a field of concern.

