# 7. Saṃskāra: memory is creative world-reconstruction

Modern memory research has long rejected the idea that memory is a perfect recording. Levin pushes the principle across scales, proposing “self-improvising memory”: memory preserves and creatively reinterprets salience rather than simply reproducing past detail with perfect fidelity. He applies this perspective from developmental biology through individual cognition and collective systems. ([MDPI][9])

This is extremely close to the concept of *saṃskāra*.

A saṃskāra is not merely a stored image. It is a disposition that alters how the next world is produced.

The past survives by changing the system’s future:

```text
past interaction
      ↓
modified disposition
      ↓
different prediction and attention
      ↓
different experienced world
      ↓
new disposition
```

The future Levinian science will discover that:

> Memory is not located in one material record. It is the system’s altered readiness to reconstruct significance.

This can occur in:

* synaptic weights;
* gene regulation;
* bioelectric patterns;
* immune responses;
* tissue structure;
* learned bodily habits;
* language;
* social institutions.

The “self” is therefore not one object carrying memories.

It is the currently active reconstruction generated by interacting layers of memory.

Tantric *kārmamala* can be reinterpreted as precisely this path dependence: the system’s previous acts narrow which futures now feel available.

