# 8. The three malas as a theory of cognitive captivity

The three *malas* are often translated as impurities, but for a modern theory they are better understood as **self-reinforcing modes of contraction**.

## Āṇavamala: the wound of insufficiency

The finite system experiences itself as incomplete.

Biologically, this is not initially pathological. A self-maintaining organism must continually obtain what it does not contain:

* energy;
* nutrients;
* information;
* safety;
* coordination.

But the human self-model can transform functional lack into existential identity:

> “I am fundamentally insufficient.”

The organism begins pursuing not a particular missing condition but an impossible completion of its self-image.

## Māyīyamala: categorical separation

The agent treats its model-generated divisions as absolute:

* self versus world;
* mine versus yours;
* observer versus observed;
* mind versus matter.

This is not the existence of differences. It is the failure to recognise that differences are relational achievements of a bounded perspective.

## Kārmamala: habitual policy capture

Past action becomes a limited repertoire of future action.

In active-inference language, the system develops overly precise priors and repeatedly samples the world in ways that confirm its established model.

It no longer merely has habits.

Its habits determine what it is capable of noticing.

This is why liberation in Abhinavagupta’s system is not simply acquiring one correct proposition.

It is recovering degrees of freedom across the entire agent-world loop.

