# 9. The next discovery: cognition navigates many spaces

Levin and Fields have argued that cognition should be studied as competent navigation in arbitrary spaces, not only literal three-dimensional movement.

A cell can navigate:

* chemical space;
* transcriptional space;
* physiological space;
* morphological space.

An organism can navigate:

* social space;
* conceptual space;
* moral space;
* imagined futures.

A 2026 paper by Hartl, Pio-Lopez, Fields and Levin proposes that cognition across natural and artificial systems involves two coupled operations: remapping an embedding space and navigating within it through iterative error minimisation. ([arXiv][10])

This is one of the strongest bridges to the 36 tattvas.

The tattvas should not be approached only as thirty-six cosmic objects stacked above one another.

They can be read as a **progressive construction of navigable reality**:

* pure possibility;
* subject–object polarity;
* agency;
* knowledge;
* temporal sequence;
* causally bounded individuality;
* cognitive faculties;
* sensory dimensions;
* material constraints.

The descent through the tattvas is a sequence in which an increasingly determinate state-space becomes available.

The scientific future will likely replace the question:

> “What is this organism made of?”

with the additional question:

> “Which spaces can this organism distinguish, remember, remap and navigate?”

That is the beginning of a genuinely substrate-independent science of mind.

