# 10. Mātṛkā: categories do not describe worlds; they help produce them

Predictive processing generally explains how sensory worlds are constructed.

Tantrāloka goes further by treating language and symbolic articulation as powers of manifestation.

The **Mātṛkā**, the matrix of phonemes or “mothers,” represents the power through which an initially undivided field becomes articulable as distinguishable contents.

This does not mean Sanskrit syllables magically create atoms in the simplistic sense.

It means that the capacity to divide, combine and name is constitutive of the world available to a linguistic consciousness.

Language changes:

* which similarities are salient;
* which differences become stable;
* which causal narratives can be formed;
* which futures can be imagined;
* which collective goals can be shared;
* which aspects of the self can be examined.

Timalsina’s studies of *aham* and *vimarśa* emphasise that subjectivity, reflexivity and expression are deeply connected in Abhinavagupta’s philosophy. The “I” is not only an internal witness; it is the centre from which experience becomes expressible and addressable. ([ResearchGate][11])

Large language models do not prove this metaphysics. But modern embedding systems vividly demonstrate that semantic relations can be encoded as navigable geometries and that altering representational organisation alters which continuations and connections become available.

The likely future discovery is:

> **Language is a control layer over cognitive state-space.**

Words do not merely report completed experience.

They reshape the attractor landscape from which future experience is generated.

