# 11. Ritual will be rediscovered as model engineering

The Tantrāloka is not merely a metaphysical book. Its philosophy is embedded in initiation, mantra, visualisation, breath, ritual action, deity systems and embodied practice. Sanderson’s historical work is essential here because it prevents modern readers from extracting a convenient idealist philosophy while ignoring the actual ritual and scriptural traditions Abhinavagupta was synthesising. ([mahavidya.ca][12])

Why would a metaphysical genius devote so much attention to ritual?

Because explicit belief is only one level of an organism.

A person may intellectually accept that the self is constructed while their:

* posture;
* breathing;
* threat responses;
* emotional predictions;
* social reflexes;
* sense of bodily ownership

continue generating the old world.

Ritual intervenes simultaneously upon multiple layers:

```text
sound
breath
attention
posture
imagery
emotion
social synchrony
conceptual meaning
identity
```

Gregory Shaw’s interpretation of Iamblichean theurgy makes a similar point in the Neoplatonic stream: embodied symbols and ritual participation can transform the soul in ways that discursive reasoning alone cannot accomplish. Angela Voss likewise presents Ficinian imagination, music and symbolic correspondences as mediating practices that reshape the practitioner’s participation in a meaningful cosmos. ([Brill][13])

The scientific future will probably strip away many historical cosmological claims while rediscovering the operational principle:

> **A ritual is a coordinated perturbation of a multilevel generative model.**

Mantra acts through sound, rhythm, respiration, prediction and semantic association.

Visualisation recruits sensory and motor systems to make absent possibilities causally active.

Initiation reorganises identity through authority, emotion, narrative and group recognition.

Ritual is not irrational belief pasted onto cognition.

It is cognition deliberately editing itself through embodied form.

