# 12. Every perception performs the five acts

Śaiva traditions describe divine activity through five acts:

```text
sṛṣṭi       manifestation
sthiti      maintenance
saṃhāra     withdrawal
tirodhāna   concealment
anugraha    disclosure or grace
```

These can be read cosmologically, but their deepest power lies in their application to every cognitive moment.

Consider seeing a cup.

### Manifestation

A possible object is separated from background activity.

### Maintenance

The object is stabilised across eye movements, lighting changes and shifting attention.

### Withdrawal

The object disappears from active presentation when attention moves elsewhere.

### Concealment

The constructed nature of “the cup” is hidden. It appears to exist simply and independently.

### Recognition

The appearance can be reapprehended as an event within the wider field of knowing.

Predictive science already models the first four reasonably well:

* hypothesis generation;
* object persistence;
* attentional removal;
* model transparency.

It has only begun to investigate the fifth.

**Anugraha** is not simply another prediction.

It is the moment in which a model becomes less transparent to itself—when the system recognises that the experienced world is its world-construction without concluding that the world is arbitrary or nonexistent.

This is a candidate scientific definition of contemplative insight:

> A change in which the generative process itself becomes available within the generated world.

