# 13. The twelve Kālīs: cognition devouring itself

The Krama doctrine of the twelve Kālīs, incorporated into Tantrāloka, is even more radical.

Scholarly studies describe Abhinavagupta’s twelve Kālīs as a phenomenology of cognitive process. The sequence moves through transformations of the object, the means of cognition and the knowing subject, culminating in their “consumption” or repossession within consciousness. ([Springer][14])

Ordinary cognition has the structure:

```text
subject → means of knowing → object
```

Science usually treats this structure as given:

* an observer;
* a measuring system;
* an observed state.

The Krama analysis asks what happens when each term is itself made available to awareness.

First, the object is seen as a manifestation.

Then the process of knowing is seen as a manifestation.

Finally the subject-position itself is seen as a manifestation.

What remains is not an additional hidden observer behind the previous observer. It is the non-objectifiable field in which the whole triad appears.

This is the likely endpoint of scientific self-modelling:

> Not a model containing an endlessly smaller model of an observer, but recognition that modelling, model and modeller are differentiations within one event.

A system cannot represent this exhaustively as one more object, because every representation recreates the subject–object distinction.

It can only **enact** the recognition.

