# 15. Frankish is right about concealment but wrong if concealment becomes nothing

Illusionism will likely explain a great deal.

It may explain why experience seems:

* simple despite complex processing;
* private despite social construction;
* continuous despite discrete and distributed mechanisms;
* intrinsic despite dependence upon action and memory;
* indescribable despite being functionally structured.

In Śaiva terminology, this is **tirodhāna**, concealment.

The cognitive machinery conceals its own machinery so that the organism can act through a usable world rather than continuously confronting its own construction process.

A tennis player sees the ball, not probability distributions and retinal transformations.

Transparency is adaptive.

But the Tantric objection is decisive:

> Concealment is itself a mode of manifestation.

The theory of illusion must explain not only why we make false judgements about phenomenal properties but why there is any appearing in which those judgements and distortions occur.

Abhinavagupta therefore gives us a stronger position than naive phenomenal realism:

* introspection is constructed;
* qualia are relationally and biologically structured;
* the self is not transparent to itself;
* nevertheless, manifestation cannot be reduced to a third-person description of what manifestation does.

