# 17. The strongest future predictions

## First: there will be atlases of enacted worlds

Science will map organisms not merely by anatomy and genes but by the dimensions of reality they can access:

* chemical distinctions;
* temporal depth;
* memory;
* controllable outcomes;
* social inference;
* abstract space;
* self-modification.

These will be maps of cognitive light cones and agent-specific worlds.

## Second: selfhood will become experimentally editable

Scientists will manipulate bioelectric and communication networks to alter the scale at which biological goals operate.

The question “What is the organism?” will become experimentally negotiable.

## Third: valence will be studied below nervous systems

Researchers will increasingly distinguish mere reaction from states that function as organism-relative benefit, threat, tension and relief.

This will force deeper analysis of minimal caring.

## Fourth: memory will be located in dynamical readiness

Memory will be understood less as a stored object and more as a system’s capacity to reconstruct salient possibilities.

## Fifth: language will be treated as morphogenesis in conceptual space

Words, narratives and symbols will be studied as mechanisms that stabilise cognitive forms just as bioelectric networks stabilise anatomical forms.

## Sixth: pathology will be reframed as conflict among agentive scales

Cancer, autoimmune processes, trauma and some psychological disorders will increasingly be modelled as disagreements between nested selves over boundaries, goals and error signals.

This will not replace molecular explanation; it will add a control-level explanation.

## Seventh: consciousness will detach from familiar anatomy

Biohybrid systems, organoids, artificial agents and chimeric organisms will force science to assess perspective without relying upon resemblance to an adult human brain. Levin’s TAME framework explicitly anticipates intelligence in evolved, engineered and hybrid embodiments. ([arXiv][17])

## Eighth: objectivity will be redefined

Objective reality will no longer mean a world as seen from nowhere.

It will mean the stable structure that survives translation among many enacted worlds.

This is structural realism made biological:

> Reality is what different perspectives cannot arbitrarily change, even though each perspective discloses it differently.

## Ninth: contemplative practices will become model interventions

Meditation and ritual will increasingly be studied according to which level they alter:

| Upāya            | Possible modern analogue                                       |
| ---------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **āṇavopāya**    | breath, posture, sensory and physiological regulation          |
| **śāktopāya**    | language, imagery, attention and generative-model revision     |
| **śāmbhavopāya** | direct modulation of intention and global orientation          |
| **anupāya**      | discontinuous recognition requiring no extended reconstruction |

These are analogies, not established neuroscientific identities. But they predict different intervention classes rather than one generic state called meditation.

