What will Levin and his successors continue to find?

They will discover that intelligence is not inside brains but distributed across nested systems of constraint and communication.

They will discover that individuality is not a fundamental object but an achieved scale of coordinated concern.

They will discover that boundaries do not merely separate; they create perspectives.

They will discover that desire, stress and value are not decorative additions to cognition but conditions for the existence of a world.

They will discover that memory reconstructs identity instead of storing it.

They will discover that language, symbols and rituals reshape the spaces through which agents navigate.

They will discover that lower-level selves must partially surrender their private goals for higher selves to emerge.

They will discover that every organism lives in a projection—but that “projection” does not mean falsehood. It means a real, constrained manifestation generated through the capacities of a particular agent.

They will discover increasingly precise correlates of integration, recursion, self-modelling and causal closure.

But none of those discoveries, by themselves, will cross from structure to presence.

At that boundary, science will have three choices:

1. declare presence an introspective fiction;
2. add presence to matter as an unexplained extra property;
3. recognise that physical structure and lived presence are complementary disclosures of one reflexive process.

Tantrāloka selects the third.

Its final claim is not merely that consciousness exists everywhere.

It is that **being, appearing and acting are inseparable**.

> *citiḥ svatantrā viśvasiddhihetuḥ*
> “Free consciousness is the ground of the manifestation of the universe.”

The future synthesis does not need to claim that the medieval cosmology has been scientifically proven.

It can make a more disciplined claim:

> Matter, as science encounters it, is relational structure capable of organising itself into bounded, value-sensitive, self-modelling agents. Experience, as encountered from within, is the intrinsic presence of such perspectival organisation. Agency is the bridge: the power by which a perspective selects, maintains and transforms a world.

The organism is therefore not a machine looking out upon the universe.

It is one of the places where the universe becomes a world.

And liberation is not the destruction of that local world.

It is the recognition that the boundary making it possible was functionally real, but never metaphysically absolute.

The next useful expansion would be a technical companion mapping the **36 tattvas, three malas, five acts, twelve Kālīs and four upāyas** onto specific hypotheses and experimental programmes in diverse intelligence research.

